==Problem==

Five runners completed the grueling Xmarathon: Luke, Melina, Nico, Olympia, and Pedro.

Nico finished <imath>11</imath> minutes behind Pedro.
Olympia finished <imath>2</imath> minutes ahead of Melina, but <imath>3</imath> minutes behind Pedro.
Olympia finished <imath>6</imath> minutes ahead of Luke.

Which runner finished fourth?

<imath>\textbf{(A) }\text{Luke} \qquad \textbf{(B) }\text{Melina} \qquad \textbf{(C) }\text{Nico} \qquad \textbf{(D) }\text{Olympia}\qquad \textbf{(E) }\text{Pedro}</imath>

==Solution 1==

Let the respective places be <imath> L </imath>, <imath> M </imath>, <imath> N </imath>, <imath> O </imath>, and <imath> P </imath> respectively. Then, <imath> P = 11+N </imath>, <imath> O-2 = M </imath>, <imath> O+3 = P </imath>, and <imath> O = 6+L </imath>. Then, substitution for <imath> P </imath> gives us <imath> O+3 = N+11 </imath>, in which <imath> O=N+8 </imath>. Then, <imath> N+6 = M </imath>, <imath> N+8=6+L \Rightarrow N+2=L </imath>. So <imath> N </imath> finished m minutes behind everyone (that was ok), and thus was last.

Now, <imath> O=6+L </imath>, in which <imath> L+9 = P </imath>, <imath> L+7 = M </imath>, and thus <imath>\boxed{\text{Luke}}</imath> finished fourth.

~Pinotation

==Solution 2==

Define points <imath>N</imath>, <imath>P</imath>, <imath>O</imath>, <imath>M</imath>, and <imath>L</imath>, as Nico, Pedro, Olympia, Melina, and Luke's times in the marathon, respectively. Fix <imath>P</imath> somewhere. Since <imath>N</imath> is <imath>11</imath> less than <imath>P</imath>, and if we draw this out, we have that Pedro is 1st, and Nico is 2nd right now. Adding in Olympia, we have that <imath>O</imath> is <imath>3</imath> less than <imath>P</imath>, meaning that the current standings is that Pedro is 1st, Olympia is 2nd, and Nico is 3rd. Adding in Melina, because she is <imath>2</imath> minutes after Olympia, she is <imath>5</imath> less than Pedro, meaning that the current standings are Pedro is 1st, Olympia is 2nd, Melina is 3rd, and Nico is 4th. Finally, adding in <imath>L</imath>, we have <imath>L</imath> is <imath>6</imath> less than <imath>O</imath>, so <imath>L</imath> is <imath>9</imath> less than <imath>P</imath>. Therefore, we have our final standings, which is Pedro at 1st, Olympia at 2nd, Melina at 3rd, <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(A) }\text{Luke}}</imath> at 4th, and Nico at 5th. ~Baihly2024

==Solution 3==
Nico finished 11 minutes behind Pedro, so we could have <imath>NP=11</imath> in the following diagram:
<asy>
draw((0,0)--(11,0));
label("$N$", (0,0), N);
label("$P$", (11,0), N);
dot((0,0));
dot((11,0));
</asy>
Olympia finished <imath>2</imath> minutes ahead of Melina, but <imath>3</imath> minutes behind Pedro, so we could have <imath>MO=2</imath> and <imath>OP=3</imath> in the following diagram:
<asy>
draw((0,0)--(11,0));
label("$N$", (0,0), N);
label("$P$", (11,0), N);
label("$M$", (6,0), N);
label("$O$", (8,0), N);
dot((0,0));
dot((11,0));
dot((6,0));
dot((8,0));
</asy>
Olympia finished <imath>6</imath> minutes in front of Luke, so we could have <imath>LO=6</imath> in the following diagram:
<asy>
draw((0,0)--(11,0));
label("$N$", (0,0), N);
label("$P$", (11,0), N);
label("$M$", (6,0), N);
label("$O$", (8,0), N);
label("$L$", (2,0), N);
dot((0,0));
dot((11,0));
dot((6,0));
dot((8,0));
dot((2,0));
</asy>
Therefore, we can see clearly that point <imath>L</imath>, or Luke, was in fourth place. Therefore, our answer is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(A) }\text{Luke}}</imath>.

~Yuhao2012

==Solution 4==
Firstly, with logic, we can deduce <imath>{Pedro}</imath> finished first. Therefore, we can use Pedro as a benchmark. In the question, it was given Olympia finished 3 minutes after Pedro. Therefore <imath>O = P - 3</imath>. Next, it is also given Nico finishes 11 minutes after Pedro, so <imath>N = P - 11</imath>. The question also says that Olympia finished 6 minutes before Luke, so <imath>L = P - 9</imath>, because Olympia finished <imath>3</imath> minutes after Pedro, and Luke finished <imath>6</imath> minutes after Olympia. Finally, the question states that Olympia finished <imath>2</imath> minutes ahead of Melina, so using the same logic as Luke's case, <imath>M = P - 5</imath>. 

Now that we have all times in terms of <imath>P</imath>, we can sort them out by greatest to least. First is obviously Pedro, second is Olympia (<imath>P - 3</imath>), third is Melina (<imath>P - 5</imath>), fourth is Luke (<imath>P - 9</imath>), and lastly we have Nico with (<imath>P - 11</imath>). Therefore, our answer to the problem is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(A) }\text{Luke}}</imath>.
~Ingeniouspeacock36

